  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM EDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nUpper troughing will remain over the eastern US for the next few \ndays as surface high pressure to our north and northwest drifts \nslowly east. This will provide us with a dry days and comfortably \nlow relative humidity values each afternoon through Tuesday. Some \npatchy fog may develop in the late night hours tonight especially \nnear rivers and lakes. \n\nBy mid week the surface high will be off to our east and northeast, \nallowing low level flow to become more southwesterly and moisture to \nincrease. Models suggest short wave energy moving across the area \nmay have enough moisture to work with for a few showers and \nthunderstorms Wednesday, especially south and mountains. By Thursday \ninto Thursday night, models show a stronger upper wave moving \nthrough the Great Lakes Region, dragging a weak cold front into our \narea. This will likely be the day with the highest coverage of \nshowers and storms. We will have to wait until we get closer to see \nabout the potential for any strong to severe storms, but right now \nthe ensemble data joint probabilities for at least 500 J/kg of CAPE \nand > 30kt bulk shear are minimal at best and the chance for severe \nstorms looks quite low. \n\nLower confidence in the details further out, but models generally \nsuggest high pressure will bring at least some drying behind the \nfront for Friday into the weekend. The NBM still has some low \nchances for showers and storms for these days, especially over the \nmountains during the afternoon hours. \n\nHigh temperatures will generally not be far either side of normal \nfor the entire forecast period.",
